<section id="faq">
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<details>
<summary>Is affiliate marketing a pyramid scheme?</summary>
<p>No. The FTC defines a pyramid scheme by multi-level compensation that rewards recruitment over product sales. Affiliate marketing is single-level — you earn only on your direct sales. There is no recruitment component in the compensation structure. See the FTC's <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/topics/protecting-consumers/pyramid-schemes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">pyramid scheme guidance</a> for the official definition.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>What is the main difference between affiliate marketing and MLM?</summary>
<p>The compensation structure. Affiliate marketing pays on one level (your direct sales only). MLM pays on multiple levels (your sales + your recruits' sales + their recruits' sales). This single difference creates fundamentally different business models — one traffic-driven, one recruitment-driven.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>Can you make money in affiliate marketing without recruiting?</summary>
<p>Yes — and this is a key difference from MLM. Affiliate marketing requires no recruitment whatsoever. Your income comes entirely from your own traffic generation and conversion skills. Many successful affiliates earn six figures without ever recruiting another person.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>Is OLSP Academy an MLM?</summary>
<p>No. We have published a <a href="/is-olsp-academy-an-mlm/">detailed investigation</a> on this question. OLSP Academy is an affiliate training platform — it teaches you how to do affiliate marketing using their Megalink system. It uses a single-tier affiliate compensation model ($5 per referral of the $7 front-end product) with no multi-level commissions or recruitment requirements.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>Which pays more — affiliate marketing or MLM?</summary>
<p>Statistically, affiliate marketing offers better income prospects for the average person. MLM income data (published by the companies themselves) shows that the vast majority of participants earn very little or lose money. Affiliate marketing income varies widely but without the structural costs (mandatory purchases, autoship fees) that eat into MLM earnings. The top earners in both models can make substantial income, but the path and probability differ dramatically.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>Are all MLMs illegal?</summary>
<p>No. Legitimate MLMs operate legally by having genuine product sales to end consumers. The FTC distinguishes legal MLMs from illegal pyramid schemes based on whether compensation is primarily tied to product sales or recruitment. However, the multi-level structure creates inherent regulatory risk, and many MLMs face legal challenges when recruitment becomes the primary income driver for participants.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>Can you do both affiliate marketing and MLM?</summary>
<p>Technically yes, but most MLM contracts include non-compete clauses that restrict promoting other products. Affiliate marketing generally has no such restrictions. If you are already doing affiliate marketing, read your MLM agreement carefully before adding it — or skip the MLM entirely and focus on scaling your affiliate business.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>What should I do if I think an 'affiliate opportunity' is actually an MLM?</summary>
<p>Use the checklist in Section 8. If the opportunity checks two or more red flags (recruitment emphasis, mandatory purchases, income boasting, multi-level compensation), treat it as an MLM. Research the company on the FTC website and consumer watchdog sites before committing any money. If it feels wrong, trust that instinct.</p>
</details>
</section>

